GIVEN A DEX MACHINE.

English Major C architectural catalogue. Target: portable standard DEX.
This document specifies a virtual execution model, not a physical processor,
a compiler, an interpreter, or permission to execute arbitrary input.
Read README.md in this directory for the coverage and evidence boundary.

REGISTERS AND VALUES.

Each invocation has its own fixed-size register frame. The code_item supplies
registers_size, ins_size and outs_size. Register indices begin at zero and
must be less than registers_size. The fact that an encoding can represent
65535 does not establish that register 65535 exists in a particular frame.
registers_size is itself a sixteen-bit count.

A register holds thirty-two bits when used for integer or floating-point
bits. An adjacent pair holds a sixty-four-bit long or double. The lower
numbered register holds the low half. Odd-numbered pair starts are allowed.
Both members must exist. A pair start selected by a four-bit field can be
v15 with v16 as its second member if the frame contains both.

An object register holds one object reference. DEX does not promise that
this reference is a portable thirty-two-bit physical address. Null has the
zero representation for the purposes of DEX constant and reference rules.
An arbitrary integer is not thereby a reference. Verification tracks types,
uninitialized references, and the relationship between the halves of pairs.
DEX registers have no ARM N/Z/C/V flags and no hardware PC register.

Read all source values from the state before writing a destination. In
particular move-wide may copy vN:vN+1 to vN-1:vN or vN+1:vN+2 without
destroying a source half before reading it. The same original-value rule
applies to permitted arithmetic source/destination aliases and two-address
operations. A write which splits another live pair changes the validity of
later uses of that pair; it does not preserve the overwritten old value.
This is not a promise of atomic shared-memory access to a pair.

FRAMES, CALLS AND RESULTS.

Incoming argument words occupy the last ins_size registers in order.
A long or double consumes two adjacent words. A non-static method receives
its receiver first. Each invocation has a fresh frame; argument registers
in the caller are read, not transferred out of existence. The frame and
outgoing-word limits must accommodate the method's prototype and call.

The invocation instructions select an ordered list of argument words.
The compact forms list up to five individual four-bit register indices;
both consecutive words of a wide argument must be listed. Range forms
select a consecutive interval by a sixteen-bit first index and an eight-bit
word count. Counts are words, not the number of mathematical arguments.
The receiver counts as one word. A zero count has an empty interval and
must not be interpreted as an access to first_register minus one.

A call suspends this frame and enters the selected method or the applicable
native method boundary. On normal completion its value, if any, is available
in a transient result channel. This is not an ordinary named DEX register.
The caller resumes immediately after its whole invocation instruction.
Only an immediately following move-result of the correct kind may retain
that value. Otherwise the result is ignored. A branch into move-result
cannot manufacture a result. A void call supplies no value to move-result.
filled-new-array also supplies an object result for move-result-object.

Return instructions leave the current invocation, perform required method
exit synchronization, and resume its caller with the declared kind of result.
They do not continue at the next instruction in the returning method.
Constructor calls additionally change the verifier's uninitialized-reference
state. Allocation and constructor invocation remain separate operations.

THE EXECUTION INDEX.

The repository's "What is the execution index?" defines the concept by the
location of the next instruction. In DEX this location is identified by the
current method together with a nonnegative offset into code_item.insns.
The unit is a sixteen-bit code unit. It is not an instruction ordinal,
a byte count, an ARM PC operand, or an address of generated native code.
A byte offset into the method's instruction array is twice this offset.

In the catalogue, current means the offset of the instruction being executed.
Normal sequential execution continues at current plus the instruction's
whole length in code units. A branch displacement is a signed count of
code units relative to current, never relative to the following instruction.
The result must designate the start of an executable instruction in the same
method. A displacement inside a multi-unit instruction or payload is invalid.
Conditional branches and goto/goto16 prohibit zero displacements. goto/32
permits zero, so it can express a self-loop. A normal path cannot fall off
the method's instruction array.

A switch's encoded displacement finds its data payload relative to the
switch instruction. The case displacements stored in that payload are also
relative to the switch instruction, not to the table or to individual entries.
On a missing case, continue immediately after the three-unit switch.
All three payload kinds begin at an even code-unit offset, that is, at a
four-byte boundary relative to the instruction array. They are data and must
never be reached by ordinary sequential or branch execution.

REFERENCES AND RESOLUTION.

An index identifies an entry in the current DEX file's string_ids, type_ids,
field_ids, method_ids, proto_ids, call_site_ids or method_handles table as
specified by the opcode. The same number in another table or file has no
implied relationship. Most indices occupy sixteen bits; const-string/jumbo
has a thirty-two-bit string index. An encoded index must be in its table.
An index is not a native pointer, field offset, vtable offset or object.

Resolution uses the referencing method's class loader and access context.
Missing definitions, wrong member kind and access restrictions can prevent
completion. Implementations may resolve eagerly or lazily within the
applicable language/runtime rules. A symbolic reference does not assert
that the referenced definition is present in this same DEX file.

Resolving or obtaining a Class object does not by itself initialize that
class. new-instance, static field access and static invocation have the
class-initialization obligations described in the companion effects document.
Array creation does not initialize its reference component class. Interning
a string, resolving a method handle, loading a class, and running a class
initializer are distinct operations.

FETCHING AND EXCEPTIONAL COMPLETION.

Identify the low opcode byte of the first code unit, decode the whole format,
and interpret operands using its width, signedness and reference kind.
The most significant byte of a 10x instruction is unused and must be zero;
0x0100, 0x0200 and 0x0300 identify payloads instead of executable nop.
Other nonzero high-byte values do not grant new portable nop meanings.
Multi-unit immediate fields place less significant units before more
significant units; decoding a file's byte order is a separate earlier step.

Normal completion applies the stated data effects and continuation.
Exceptional completion does not also perform normal continuation or write
an unproduced normal result. Effects already made by invoked code, class
initialization, or other threads are not rolled back. A throw searches the
applicable protected instruction range, then its handlers, or unwinds to
the caller if no matching handler exists. A caller's handler search uses
the calling instruction, not a successfully completed return continuation.
An uncaught exception leaves the current call chain through the runtime's
uncaught-exception boundary. This does not prescribe an Android UI response.

At handler entry the caught object is available to an initial move-exception.
Such an instruction is allowed only as the handler's first instruction.
A handler may ignore the object and omit it. Ordinary control flow must not
enter a move-exception and pretend that an exception has just been caught.
monitor-exit has the explicitly documented exceptional-index exception:
its exception is attributed as though the index had advanced past it.

LAYERS WHICH MUST REMAIN SEPARATE.

Encoding validity asks whether the bytes contain the right format, unused
bits, field widths, boundaries, table indices, lengths and alignment.
Verification additionally checks register types, pairs, prototypes,
uninitialized objects, return categories, legal result/handler positions,
branch joins and monitor discipline. Execution additionally depends on actual
nullness, bounds, loaded definitions, access, initialization, locks and memory.
An invalid method is not granted a successful runtime meaning merely because
its opcode number is standard or an independent decoder prints a mnemonic.

ART's interpreter, JIT, ahead-of-time compiler, caches, compressed references,
garbage collection barriers and quickening are implementations. This catalogue
does not select one. Android Activities, Binder, widgets and framework APIs
are library/service behavior reached by calls, not extra DEX instructions.
Generated ARM instructions have their own machine contract. DEX v0 and ARM R0
are different objects even when one lowering happens to use one for the other.

